    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Learning to Balance – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Learning to Balance</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> How to Balance Social and School Activities</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Comprehend complex narrative texts. Reading: Analyze persuasive pieces on current events.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
            <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can comprehend narratives about balancing school, social activities, and personal life (e.g., "The speaker described how they balance their academic work with social activities like volunteering. They explained how managing time well helps them stay organized and reduce stress.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can analyze persuasive pieces about managing relaxation effectively (e.g., "The article argues that students who set clear boundaries between school and personal time tend to have lower stress levels.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can discuss the benefits of balancing school work and relaxation (e.g., "The story suggests that having time for both schoolwork and relaxation helps students stay focused.")</li>
            </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by projecting three images: one showing a student studying late, another showing friends at a social gathering, and a third showing someone practicing a hobby. Students are asked to briefly discuss in pairs what they see and how these images relate to their own lives. The teacher then writes the word "BALANCE" on the board and asks students what comes to mind when they see it, linking it to *school activities* and *social life*. The teacher prompts: "What *have you been doing* to keep a *healthy balance* between your *workload* and *relaxation*?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading a short sentence related to the theme, focusing on pronunciation. For example: "Managing time for school *activities* can help *relieve* *stress*." The teacher highlights and exaggerates the short /ɪ/ sound in "activities" /ækˈtɪvɪtɪz/ and the long /iː/ sound in "relieve" /rɪˈliːv/. The teacher writes key vocabulary from Lesson 1 on the board (e.g., *stress*, *relaxation*, *schedule*, *prioritize*, *wellbeing*) and models their pronunciation, ensuring correct stress and intonation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the warm-up topic and key vocabulary.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'balance' about doing only schoolwork or only social *activities*? (Expected answer: No, it's about doing both.)"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean to 'relieve stress'? Is it to make *stress* worse or better? (Expected answer: To make it better, less.)"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Learning to Balance".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher distributes a handout with an excerpt from a blog post titled "My Journey to a *Balanced* Life: Overcoming *Procrastination*." The text is a complex narrative and contains persuasive elements about the importance of *time management* and setting *boundaries*. It features target vocabulary such as *balance, routine, activities, hobbies, schedule, flexibility, stress, relaxation, mindfulness, wellbeing, social life, workload, time management, downtime, procrastination, prioritize, manage, organize, set goals, relax, unwind, focus, relieve* and grammatical structures like the present perfect continuous and second conditional. (Genre: Blog post excerpt, Register: Informal to semi-formal, Format: Written text).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading Comprehension – Students will comprehend complex narrative texts and identify persuasive arguments. They will understand the main ideas and specific details related to *balancing* academic, social, and personal *activities*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Students read the blog post silently for 3 minutes. Then, the teacher asks: "What is the main challenge the author *has been* trying to overcome?" and "What is the author's overall message about *workload* and *wellbeing*?" (Students share answers orally).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students reread the text to determine if statements are true or false, correcting the false ones. For example: "The author *has been* consistently following a *flexible routine* since high school." or "If the author *had* more *downtime*, they *would* completely stop all *social activities*."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying and interpreting target grammar (Present perfect continuous, Second conditional, Modals for possibility) and vocabulary (*balance, routine, activities, schedule, boundaries, stress, relaxation, time management, procrastinate, relieve*) within the context of the reading passage. They will focus on accurate reading comprehension and interpretation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice:</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with sentences extracted from the blog post where target vocabulary words or grammatical structures are missing. Students fill in the blanks, choosing from a word bank (e.g., *prioritize, wellbeing, schedule, boundaries, has been studying, would feel, might*). Example: "The author ___________ (study) hard to manage her *workload*." "If students ___________ (set) clear *boundaries*, they ___________ (feel) less *overwhelming*." Students also identify sentences in the text that show *present perfect continuous* and *second conditional* and underline them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled substitution drill (Reading for details):</b> In pairs, students read specific paragraphs again to find examples of the author giving suggestions. They identify sentences using modals (e.g., "You *might* need to set aside time for *mindfulness*." "Students *should* *plan* their *schedule* carefully."). The teacher then asks them to paraphrase these suggestions or rephrase hypothetical situations from the text using their own words but maintaining the grammatical structure. For example, "The author suggests that if someone *had been feeling* overwhelmed, they *could* try to *allocate* time for *hobbies*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction/peer check (Pronunciation focus):</b> Students take turns reading aloud short sentences or phrases from the text that contain the /ɪ/ sound (e.g., "activities," "flexibility," "mindfulness") and the /iː/ sound (e.g., "relieve," "routine," "wellbeing"). They provide peer feedback on clarity and pronunciation, specifically focusing on distinguishing these vowel sounds. The teacher circulates, offering corrective feedback and reinforcing the sounds.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Learning to Balance".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Case Study Analysis (Reading-to-Discussion):</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a short, anonymized case study (a paragraph) describing a 12th-grade student struggling with *balancing* school, social life, and personal *wellbeing*. The case study integrates the target grammar and vocabulary. Groups read their case study, then discuss and collaboratively formulate advice for the student in the case study. They must *give suggestions* using modals of possibility (e.g., "This student *might* benefit from...", "They *should* try to *prioritize*...", "If they *had* a clearer *schedule*, they *could* *relieve* some *stress*.") and *express opinions* on the student's situation (e.g., "I think the student *has been* *overwhelming* themselves...", "In my opinion, they *should* set *boundaries*...").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme and Scenario:</b> This task directly addresses "How to Balance Social and School Activities" by requiring students to analyze a real-world scenario (even if hypothetical) and apply strategies discussed in the reading text. It supports "Learning to Balance" by having students articulate practical solutions. Groups are encouraged to incorporate sociolinguistic aspects by discussing diverse approaches to *time management* and *relaxation* based on their own cultural contexts, encouraging inclusive communication.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in discussing the case study based on their reading, collaborative interaction, and communicative task completion through formulating and presenting coherent advice. Emphasis is placed on using the target grammar and vocabulary accurately to give suggestions and express opinions.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Observation Checklist & Group Presentation:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to assess each group's collaborative discussion and their ability to formulate clear advice. Each group briefly presents their advice for their case study to the class (1-2 minutes per group), focusing on the strategies they read about and their own suggestions. The teacher assesses their comprehension of the text and their application of the target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students collectively present their solutions and advice for their assigned case study. This oral presentation demonstrates their ability to comprehend the reading, analyze the situation, and communicate informed *suggestions* and *opinions* using the target vocabulary and grammatical structures (Present perfect continuous, Second conditional, Modals for possibility).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Accuracy in understanding the case study and the main blog post's themes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Effective use of words like *balance, schedule, boundaries, stress, relaxation, time management, prioritize, relieve* (B1.3).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Grammar:</b> Correct application of Present perfect continuous (e.g., "The student *has been feeling* overwhelmed."), Second conditional (e.g., "If they *allocated* more *downtime*, they *would relieve* *stress*."), and Modals for possibility for suggestions (e.g., "They *should* *manage* their *schedule* better.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, with an emphasis on distinguishing /ɪ/ in *activities* and /iː* in *relieve*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Coherent presentation of advice and logical flow of ideas.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active and inclusive participation in group discussion and presentation.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new strategy for *time management* or *relaxation* you learned from today's reading or discussion?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel in identifying the main idea of a complex text about *balancing* *activities*? (1-5 scale)"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's lesson (reading the blog, discussing, or giving advice) helped you understand the challenges of *balancing* *activities* the most, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *might* you apply a strategy from the reading to improve your own *schedule* or *wellbeing* this week?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The reading text was appropriate for B1.3 level, and students engaged well with analyzing the content and formulating advice. The emphasis on pronunciation of /ɪ/ and /iː/ was successful during peer reading. Some groups needed more prompting to consistently use the present perfect continuous and second conditional in their advice, relying more on simple present and future forms. For the next lesson, I will provide sentence starters or explicit reminders for using these target grammatical structures more naturally in spoken interaction. I also need to ensure more equitable participation during group discussions, perhaps by assigning specific roles or requiring each member to contribute a particular type of sentence.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and interpreting strategies for *balance*. The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will transition to students actively discussing these strategies, sharing personal experiences, and giving and receiving advice in more dynamic spoken interactions, building on the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today. They will *express opinions* and *give suggestions* more spontaneously.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>